\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The Field Origin as a vortex core. \textbf  {Left:} A river whirlpool viewed from the side. Water spirals inward at the surface, passes through the central axis, and disperses below. At the vortex core, rotation vanishes but through-flow continues. \textbf  {Right:} The dual tetrahedral structure. Angular momentum spirals inward through TetA, passes through the Field Origin where $t = 0$, and spirals outward through TetB. At both vortex cores, rotation $\to 0$ but flow continues through. At the Field Origin, proper time $\tau \to 0$, yet angular momentum flows through, yielding the observed spin $\hbar /2$.}}{21}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:vortex}{{2}{21}{The Field Origin as a vortex core. \textbf {Left:} A river whirlpool viewed from the side. Water spirals inward at the surface, passes through the central axis, and disperses below. At the vortex core, rotation vanishes but through-flow continues. \textbf {Right:} The dual tetrahedral structure. Angular momentum spirals inward through TetA, passes through the Field Origin where $t = 0$, and spirals outward through TetB. At both vortex cores, rotation $\to 0$ but flow continues through. At the Field Origin, proper time $\tau \to 0$, yet angular momentum flows through, yielding the observed spin $\hbar /2$}{figure.2}{}}
